[R0295] year=2026 | venue=Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies
TITLE: DESIGN OF A HYBRID INFORMATION SECURITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM BASED ON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TECHNOLOGIES
FULL-TEXT EXCERPT: Information and controlling system The process of managing cybersecurity operations that include continuous monitoring, detection, analysis, and response to cyber threats, aimed at protecting the organization's digital assets, has been investigated in this study. The task addressed is to improve the efficiency and speed of automated incident response by combining deterministic pre-filtering tools with the cognitive capabilities of language models (LLMs). In the course of the research, an architecture of a multi-agent information security management system (MAS-ISM) has been designed, which operates on the basis of decentralized coalition auctions and a hybrid risk analysis subsystem (Sandwich Engine) for express search of incidents in system event logs for automated generation of expert reports in the state language using the generation technology supplemented by Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and LLM search. The solution to the tasks of obtaining technical cybersecurity reports in the state language and recommendations for further decision-making by the analyst has been shown. The main difference of the hybrid risk assessment system from existing ones is the use of deterministic retrospective session isolation, which made it possible to ensure data confidentiality, automatic expert description of the current state of cybersecurity in the national language, and the compilation of recommendations for analysts. The research results are attributed to the combined use of AI methods: RAG technology, deterministic retrospective session isolation, and LLM. The total time for analyzing one complex incident and forming a structured expert SOC report in the state language was reduced to 12 seconds (compared to 15–45 minutes under traditional manual analysis), while guaranteeing the confidentiality of critical data from leakage into the public cloud circuit Keywords: cybersecurity, pre-filtration, LLM, multi-agent systems, event logs, Retrieval-Augmented Generation, hybrid systems UDC 004.056:004.896:510.644 DOI: 10.15587/1729-4061.2026.368661 DESIGN OF A HYBRID INFORMATION SECURITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM BASED ON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TECHNOLOGIES Mykhailo Mozhaiev Doctor of Technical Sciences, Senior Researcher, Associate Professor Department of Electronic Computers Kharkiv National University of Radioelectronics Nauky ave., 14, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61166 ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1566-9260 Inna Khavina Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1856-1186 Oleksandr Mozhaiev Corresponding author Doctor of Technical Sciences, Professor* E-mail: mozhaev1957@gmail.com ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1412-2696 Volodymyr Strukov Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor Department of Mathematical Modeling and Data Analysis V. N. Karazin Kharkiv National University Svobody sq., 4, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61022 ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4722-3159 Yurii Gnusov Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9017-9635 Mykhailo Tsuranov Senior Lecturer* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2115-7029 *Department of Cyber Security and DATA Technologies Kharkiv National University of Internal Affairs L. Landau ave., 27, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61080 Received 12.05.2026 Received in revised form 20.07.2026 Accepted date 29.07.2026 Published date 28.08.2026 How to Cite: Mozhaiev, O., Khavina, I., Strukov, V., Mozhaiev, M., Gnusov, Y., Tsuranov, M. (2026). Development of a hybrid information security management system based on artificial intelligence technologies. Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies, 4 (9 (142)), 33–46. https://doi.org/10.15587/1729-4061.2026.368661 1. Introduction The modern world is heavily dependent on information technology, which makes cybersecurity extremely important [1]. Various cyberattacks are becoming increasingly complex, threatening both individual users and entire organizations. The current cyberthreat landscape is characterized by a rapid increase in the complexity and intensity of attacks, in particular, zero-day threats and combined fan intrusions. Automated systems for managing information system (IS) events, SIEM systems, are used to solve the task of controlling the flow of events coming from integrated security control systems and to computerize the incident management process. These systems require frequent tuning and adaptation to fully and correctly perform their tasks, as they operate 33 Copyright © 2026 Authors. This is an open access article under the Creative Commons CC BY license Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies ISSN-L 1729-3774; E-ISSN 1729-4061 4/9 ( 142 ) 2026 in a constantly changing, dynamic environment. They collect events in real time, analyze them in near real time, send notifications and display information on the operator console for faster implementation of protective measures. SIEM systems play a critical role in the diverse workflows of analysts at Security Operations Centers (SOCs). However, modern SIEM and AIOps platforms that use machine learning and Big Data to optimize IT operations face a major challenge: they still cannot free analysts from the constant tasks of analyzing and writing technical reports in natural languages and significant incident response times. State-of-the-art LLMs have demonstrated significant capabilities in interpreting unstructured data, generating threat descriptions, and even interacting with security environments through thought chains and augmented search generation. Big language models, using data sets and advanced neural network architectures, have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in understanding and generating human language. Thus, the use of big language models (LLMs) fundamentally changes approaches to cybersecurity, allowing for the analysis of unstructured data sets and the interpretation of the context of events directly in real time. However, despite the high analytical potential of LLMs, their direct application in response systems is limited by the high cost of computing resources, the “black box” issue, and the lack of mechanisms for a clear mathematical justification of decisions made (explainability of the result). Therefore, it is urgent to build a hybrid information security management system based on artificial intelligence technologies using a synergy of deterministic analysis and stochastic modeling methods. Such a system could reduce the time for incident analysis, fully ensure data confidentiality, as well as significantly reduce the risk of artificial intelligence errors (hallucinations) when using the state language. The hybrid approach involves the infrastructural and algorithmic combination of the following: – a local contour of the protected perimeter, which, based on deterministic mathematical logic, the TF-IDF algorithm, and finite automata, performs express search, information “noise” filtering, and two-way irreversible anonymizatio [...] 01683. https://doi.org/10.1016/ j.fsidi.2023.301683 14. Braquet, M., Bakolas, E. (2021). Greedy Decentralized Auction-based Task Allocation for Multi-Agent Systems. IFAC-PapersOnLine, 54 (20), 675–680. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ifacol.2021.11.249 15. Yi, J. (2023). Regret-Minimization Algorithms for Multi-Agent Cooperative Learning Systems. arXiv. https://doi.org/10.48550/ arXiv.2310.19468 16. Khavina, I. (2016). Multiagent system for the optimal control of a combination of transport robots in an manufacturing technological process. 2016 13th International Conference on Modern Problems of Radio Engineering, Telecommunications and Computer Science (TCSET), 468–472. https://doi.org/10.1109/tcset.2016.7452090 17. Yang, S. (2025). Social Media User Behavior Modeling and Content Distribution Optimization Using GAT and Temporal Graph Networks. Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence of Things and Computing, 218–222. https:// doi.or